    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the change. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The pentatonic layer",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-pentatonic-e-form"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " packed this window's twelve dots onto only three frets — "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "8",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — with nothing at fret 4 and nothing at fret 6. The scale puts exactly one dot on each of those two frets: "
        },
        {
          "text": "3·4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", a "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", and "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", a "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Two notes turn chapter 3's most compact window into a shape that fills all five frets — of the five windows, this one's dots grew the most in span, three frets to five."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Those two frets weren't empty by accident. Fret 4 carries exactly one note of A natural minor anywhere on the neck, and fret 6 carries exactly one too — the two sparsest non-empty frets there are, checked fret by fret rather than assumed. Both of those single notes are new at this layer, and the E form is the only one of the five windows that contains both of those frets. The G form has fret 4 and stops there; the D form has fret 6 and stops there; only this window has both."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The pair on string 2 is the chapter's central move, and it sits inside the most-played window on the instrument. "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "; "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", one fret above it, on the string right under the barre finger. Hold the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " barre at fret 5 and add "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — that's the note the pentatonic never gave you. Let it fall back to "
        },
        {
          "text": "2·5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and feel it settle."
        }
      ]
    },
